  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through next Friday)\nIssued at 1106 PM CDT FRI SEP 18 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery late this evening reveals our well advertised\ninverted trough slowly moving across Florida. A plume of slightly\ndeeper moisture wrapped around the northern side of this feature\nled to a few isolated showers and storms across the region this \nafternoon. It is likely that this feature becomes a closed low \nbefore emerging over the Gulf. As it scoots west, it will nudge \nthe upper level ridge back towards the Plains. This will open up a\nwindow for some low to medium chances for showers and storms over\nthe weekend and into next week. Greatest chances daily will come \nduring the afternoon to early evening hours as diurnal heating \nkicks in. While coverage will be isolated on Saturday, we could \nsee a couple strong to marginally severe thunderstorms capable of \nproducing damaging winds. As a result, SPC currently has eastern \ncentral Alabama outlooked in a Marginal (Level 1 out of 5) risk \nfor severe thunderstorms. Similar conditions are in store for \nSunday as the upper low inches across the eastern Gulf. As we head\ntowards the middle of the work week, the upper low deteriorates. \nHowever, ample moisture left in place will lead to diurnally \ndriven convection.\n\nTemperatures look to remain warm over the coming days with highs\nin the mid to upper 90s. A few locations across the western half\nof the area are likely to touch 100 degrees again on Saturday.\nHeat indices on Saturday are likely to top out in the 105-\n107 degree range. Therefore, a Heat Advisory remains in effect\nfrom noon through 8 PM for most of Central Alabama. Increased\nrain chances/cloud cover next week will knock several degrees off\nour highs with temps settling into the upper 80s to low 90s by\nWednesday.\n\n95/Castillo",
